.TH AGENT-HOOK-TELEMETRY 1 "2026-10-07" "agentguard" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
agent-hook-telemetry \- record an agent hook event in the audit trail
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B agent-hook-telemetry
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B agent-hook-telemetry
reads any hook JSON payload from standard input and records it, unmodified,
as an
.B event
record in the session's audit directory under
.IR ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/agentguard/telemetry/sessions/ .
It always prints an empty JSON response and exits 0: it never blocks, warns,
or injects context, so it is safe to register beside any guard hook.
.PP
AgentGuard's native integrations register it for every supported event with
a match-all tool matcher, so every prompt, tool input, tool output, and
lifecycle event is captured, including tools no guard inspects. Every other
.B agent-hook-*
entry point writes a companion
.B hook
record with its decision (exit status, block and warning messages, injected
context) but not the payload.
.PP
When the payload looks like a session start, a background pass deletes
sessions idle for longer than
.B AGENTGUARD_TELEMETRY_RETENTION_DAYS
days.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B AGENTGUARD_TELEMETRY
Set to 0, false, no, or off to disable recording.
.TP
.B AGENTGUARD_TELEMETRY_DIR
Absolute path of the telemetry root; relative values are ignored.
.TP
.B AGENTGUARD_TELEMETRY_RETENTION_DAYS
Retention window in days (default 90); 0 keeps every session.
.TP
.B AGENTGUARD_TELEMETRY_MAX_PAYLOAD_CHARS
Payload length above which only a prefix is kept (default 8388608).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-~/.local/state}/agentguard/telemetry/sessions/AGENT/SESSION/*.json
One JSON record per hook invocation, owner-only. The schema is documented in
.I docs/telemetry.md
in the AgentGuard checkout.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Always 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agentguard-telemetry (1)
